# DISCOVERY, HELD AS A RECORD.
#
# The hardest part of building an application is not writing it — it is
# finding out what it is. That work happens today in a conversation nobody
# keeps: someone asks good questions for an hour, half the answers reach a
# whiteboard, and the other half becomes a decision nobody can later
# explain. The bluebook that comes out of it is an artefact with no
# provenance.
#
# This chapter is the conversation itself, modelled — the same way banking
# models a transfer rather than trusting a bank statement. What was asked,
# what came back, what is now established, and what each of those facts
# would CHANGE about the bluebook under construction, each change
# individually acceptable and each acceptance the thing that causes a write.
#
# WHAT THIS CHAPTER DOES NOT MODEL: aggregates, commands, value objects.
# The partial domain being discovered is held as META-DOMAIN RECORDS,
# dispatched into a runtime built from `lib/hecksagain/language/bluebook/` —
# the language already holds a bluebook and gives it back. A Proposal here
# NAMES a dispatch; it never restates what the dispatch would build. A
# second, parallel model of an aggregate is a second place to be wrong, and
# the language already has the first one.
#
# THE MODEL UNDER CONSTRUCTION IS DISPOSABLE, THIS IS NOT. The meta-domain
# stays in memory so a shape can be tried and thrown away with no era, no
# migration, and no gate — `EraCheck` only runs for lineage-capable
# adapters, and Memory is not one. THIS chapter is the durable half: the
# record of what was asked and decided, which is what makes an in-flight
# model rebuildable at all (re-dispatch the accepted proposals; a head is a
# projection of a journal).
#
# ATTACHED, LIKE ITS SIBLINGS, via `uses_framework "Interview"`. It
# references no other framework member — see `IdentityId` for why.
Hecks.bluebook "Interview" do
  vision "A discovery session is not a meeting anyone remembers — it is a record. Every question keeps where it came from, every fact keeps the answer it rests on, and every proposed change to the bluebook is accepted one at a time, by name, or not at all."

  # CORE, where both siblings in this directory say `supporting`, and the
  # departure is deliberate. Identity and Governance are things every domain
  # needs and no domain is judged on. This is the opposite: discovery is
  # where the work is won or lost, which is the same sentence banking's own
  # `core` stands on.
  core

  # THE SESSION. Named `Interview` inside a chapter named `Interview`, so a
  # verb reads `Interview::Interview.Ask` — the doubling identity.bluebook
  # already carries (`Identity::Identity.Register`), accepted for the same
  # reason: the aggregate IS what the chapter is about, and renaming it to
  # dodge the repetition would name it something it is not.
  aggregate "Interview" do
    description "One discovery session held against one chapter — the questions asked, the answers given, and how far it got."

    identified_by { session.value }

    attribute :session,    SessionReference
    attribute :subject,    Subject
    attribute :chapter,    ChapterName
    attribute :respondent, IdentityId
    # WHY A COUNTER, when the questions are right there in a list. The
    # predicate sublanguage has no quantifier and cannot see inside a list
    # element — `questions.any? { ... }` is not a thing a `given` can say,
    # because there are no blocks in it at all. A rule that needs to know how
    # many questions were asked needs the number HELD, exactly the way
    # ScheduledPayment holds `attempts` so `Retry`'s own given has something
    # to read. `Conclude` below is the rule this exists for.
    attribute :asked,      AskedCount
    attribute :questions,  list_of(Question)

    value_object "SessionReference" do
      attribute :value, String
      invariant("a session is referenced") { !value.to_s.empty? }
    end

    value_object "Subject" do
      attribute :value, String
      invariant("an interview names what it is about") { !value.to_s.empty? }
    end

    # THE CHAPTER BEING BUILT — the `Hecks.bluebook "…"` name every
    # Proposal's dispatch ends up qualified by. Held once on the session
    # rather than restated on every proposal: an interview works on one
    # chapter, and a proposal naming a different one would be a proposal
    # from a different interview.
    value_object "ChapterName" do
      attribute :value, String
      invariant("an interview names the chapter it is building") { !value.to_s.empty? }
    end

    # DECLARED HERE, NOT REFERENCED FROM `Identity` — the same duplication
    # governance.bluebook already makes, for the same reason. A framework
    # member is attached on its own, and nothing says the domain attaching
    # this one also attached "Identity". A `reference_to` across framework
    # members would be a load-order requirement stated nowhere and
    # discovered at a caller's first dispatch.
    value_object "IdentityId" do
      attribute :value, String
      invariant("an identity id is present") { !value.to_s.empty? }
    end

    value_object "AskedCount" do
      attribute :value, Integer, default: 0
      invariant("a question count is not negative") { !value.negative? }
    end

    value_object "QuestionSequence" do
      attribute :value, Integer
      invariant("a question sequence is positive") { value.positive? }
    end

    value_object "QuestionText" do
      attribute :value, String
      invariant("a question asks something") { !value.to_s.empty? }
    end

    value_object "AnswerText" do
      attribute :value, String
      invariant("an answer says something") { !value.to_s.empty? }
    end

    # A PLAIN WORD, NOT A CLOSED SET — banking's `Tag` carries the identical
    # comment for the identical reason: an interviewer names a topic before
    # the language has a word for it, and a scheme this narrow would refuse
    # the very session it exists to organise. Declared again on Finding and
    # StandingQuestion the way banking declares `Narrative` three times: a
    # value object belongs to one aggregate, and three aggregates that each
    # need the shape each say it.
    value_object "Topic" do
      attribute :value, String
      invariant("a topic is named") { !value.to_s.empty? }
    end

    # WHICH STANDING QUESTION THIS CAME FROM, when one did. NOT
    # `optional: true`, which is what it obviously wants to be: `Ask`'s own
    # given reads this field, and a `given` that reads an argument nobody
    # supplied does not refuse — it fails to resolve. The field is always
    # present and may be the empty string, and the rule reads it.
    value_object "CatalogueRef" do
      attribute :value, String
    end

    # THE THREE PLACES A QUESTION CAN COME FROM, and there is no fourth.
    #
    # This is the closed set in this chapter that earns the word, and it is
    # worth being precise about what it is NOT: it is not the catalogue. The
    # CATALOGUE grows — that is the whole point of having one, and it lives
    # below as `StandingQuestion`, an aggregate, which is to say data. This
    # is the layering itself, which is a design decision and does not grow:
    #
    #   mechanical — the IR already says something is missing. An aggregate
    #     with no identity, a lifecycle state nothing reaches. `model_check`
    #     and the language FIND these; the interview only asks about them.
    #   catalogue  — a StandingQuestion the practice always asks, whether or
    #     not anything looks wrong.
    #   judgment   — the interviewer noticed something no rule would have.
    #     The one source that cannot be reproduced, and so the one most
    #     worth being able to count.
    #
    # Declared the long way rather than the inline `one_of(...)` shorthand:
    # the shorthand calls `one_of` on the ENCLOSING builder, and inside a
    # `value_object` that is the value object's OWN `one_of` — a real
    # wrong-arity collision pizzas.bluebook hit while being written.
    value_object "QuestionSource" do
      attribute :value, String

      one_of do
        member value: "mechanical"
        member value: "catalogue"
        member value: "judgment"
      end
    end

    # THE TRANSCRIPT IS NOT A SEPARATE THING. It is this list read in
    # sequence order: what was asked, what came back, and what state the
    # question is in now. Modelling a transcript beside it would be a second
    # copy of a fact these already hold, and a copy nothing compares is a
    # copy that drifts.
    #
    # AN ENTITY, and the test is the one entities.md states: a question has
    # its own identity, its own commands and its own lifecycle, but never
    # makes sense apart from the session that asked it. Nobody looks up
    # question 7 without already knowing which interview asked it — the same
    # sentence that makes a bank's key an entity of the box it was cut for.
    #
    # And the thing that would argue for a separate aggregate — "but I need
    # every open question across every session" — is not an argument: an
    # entity `query` answers with every element that matches across every
    # parent, each row stamped with the parent it came from. `StillOpen` is
    # that ask.
    entity "Question" do
      description "One question put to the respondent, and the answer it did or did not get."

      # ONE PATH, where the session needs its own. The interview is already
      # known by the time anyone is looking at a question, so the number
      # within it is the whole identity — KeyIssuance's shape, not Visit's.
      #
      # NOT SUPPLIED BY THE CALLER. `Ask` does not pass `sequence`, and
      # `MutationApplier#entity_element` mints it as `size + 1` when an
      # append omits the identity — the way ATMCard's `Withdraw` never
      # numbers its own withdrawals. An interview numbers its questions in
      # the order it asked them, which is the only order that could be
      # right, and no caller can collide with itself by guessing.
      identified_by { sequence.value }

      attribute :sequence,      QuestionSequence
      attribute :text,          QuestionText
      attribute :source,        QuestionSource
      attribute :topic,         Topic
      attribute :catalogue_ref, CatalogueRef
      # THE ANSWER LIVES ON THE QUESTION and is not a thing of its own. It
      # has no identity, no lifecycle, and is never addressed — you address
      # the QUESTION, and `Answer` is the verb. That is the value-object end
      # of the three-way test arrived at from the other side: not even a
      # list element, just a field that is empty until it is not.
      attribute :answer,        AnswerText, optional: true

      lifecycle :state, default: "open" do
        transition "Answer"   => "answered",  from: "open"
        transition "Defer"    => "deferred",  from: "open"
        # AN ANSWER CAN BE WRONG. A later answer contradicts an earlier one,
        # or the respondent says "actually, go back to that" — and the
        # question is open again, with the answer that was wrong still on
        # it. The transcript keeps what was said; the state says whether it
        # settled anything.
        transition "Reopen"   => "open",      from: ["answered", "deferred"]
        transition "Withdraw" => "withdrawn", from: ["open", "deferred"]
      end

      command "Answer" do
        role "Respondent"
        goal "Say what is true, so the question stops being open"

        attribute :sequence, QuestionSequence
        attribute :answer,   AnswerText

        then_set :answer, to: :answer

        emits "QuestionAnswered"
      end

      # DEFERRED AND WITHDRAWN ARE NOT THE SAME FACT, and collapsing them
      # would lose the distinction that matters. "Not now" is a queue the
      # interviewer comes back to; "this question was wrong" is a question
      # that must never be asked again — a mechanical gap filled another
      # way, a catalogue entry that does not apply to this domain. One
      # belongs in the next session's opening; the other must not appear.
      command "Defer" do
        role "Respondent"
        goal "Park a question that cannot be answered yet"

        attribute :sequence, QuestionSequence

        emits "QuestionDeferred"
      end

      command "Reopen" do
        role "Interviewer"
        goal "Put a question back in play when its answer did not hold"

        attribute :sequence, QuestionSequence

        emits "QuestionReopened"
      end

      command "Withdraw" do
        role "Interviewer"
        goal "Take back a question that turned out not to apply"

        attribute :sequence, QuestionSequence

        emits "QuestionWithdrawn"
      end

      # ACROSS EVERY SESSION, not just this one — an entity query answers
      # with every element that matches whichever parent holds it, and
      # stamps the parent onto each row, so an answer spanning several
      # sessions still says which one each question belongs to. This is the
      # ask that made a separate `Question` aggregate unnecessary.
      query "StillOpen" do
        description "Every question waiting on an answer — the interviewer's next move."
        where(state: "open")
        order_by :sequence
      end

      query "Deferred" do
        description "Parked questions, which are the opening of the next session."
        where(state: "deferred")
        order_by :sequence
      end
    end

    lifecycle :status, default: "gathering" do
      # A SELF-TRANSITION, so "you cannot ask a question of a paused or
      # concluded interview" costs no predicate. The lifecycle already
      # refuses it, naming the states `Ask` moves from, and a `given`
      # restating that would be decoration.
      transition "Ask"      => "gathering", from: "gathering"
      transition "Pause"    => "paused",    from: "gathering"
      transition "Resume"   => "gathering", from: "paused"
      transition "Conclude" => "concluded", from: ["gathering", "paused"]
      transition "Abandon"  => "abandoned", from: ["gathering", "paused"]
    end

    # NO `then_set` ON A CREATING COMMAND — the shape both siblings in this
    # directory already use. `assign_creation_attributes` writes every
    # argument that names a head attribute, so restating them is a list to
    # keep in sync for no gain.
    command "Open" do
      role "Interviewer"
      goal "Begin a discovery session against a chapter"

      attribute :session,    SessionReference
      attribute :subject,    Subject
      attribute :chapter,    ChapterName
      attribute :respondent, IdentityId

      emits "InterviewOpened"
    end

    command "Ask" do
      role "Interviewer"
      goal "Put a question to the respondent and keep where it came from"

      reference_to Interview
      attribute :text,          QuestionText
      attribute :source,        QuestionSource
      attribute :topic,         Topic
      attribute :catalogue_ref, CatalogueRef

      # IMPLICATION, SPELLED THE ONLY WAY THE SUBLANGUAGE HAS: `!A || B`. A
      # catalogue question that cannot name its catalogue entry is not a
      # catalogue question, it is judgment wearing a source it did not earn
      # — and the whole value of `source` is that the three populations can
      # be counted apart afterwards.
      given("a catalogue question names the entry it came from") do
        source.value != "catalogue" || !catalogue_ref.value.to_s.empty?
      end

      # `sequence` IS ABSENT ON PURPOSE — see Question's own identity. The
      # runtime numbers it.
      then_set :questions, append: { text: :text, source: :source, topic: :topic, catalogue_ref: :catalogue_ref }
      then_set :asked,     increment: { value: 1 }

      ensures("asking never loses a question") { asked.value == old.asked.value + 1 }

      emits "QuestionAsked"
    end

    command "Pause" do
      role "Interviewer"
      goal "Stop a session that will be picked back up"

      reference_to Interview
      emits "InterviewPaused"
    end

    command "Resume" do
      role "Interviewer"
      goal "Pick a paused session back up"

      reference_to Interview
      emits "InterviewResumed"
    end

    command "Conclude" do
      role "Interviewer"
      goal "End a session whose findings and proposals are worth acting on"

      reference_to Interview

      # THE ONE RULE THE COUNTER EXISTS FOR. An interview that asked nothing
      # discovered nothing, and concluding it would put a session in the
      # record a later reader would take as evidence the chapter had been
      # examined. It has not been.
      #
      # "And nothing is still open" is the rule this WANTED to be, and it
      # cannot be written: it needs a count over the elements of a list
      # filtered by their own state, and the sublanguage has no quantifier
      # and no blocks. A second counter would not rescue it either — a
      # Question's commands are ENTITY commands, and an entity command's
      # mutations land on the entity, never on the head that holds it, so
      # nothing could decrement one. `StillOpen` is where that question is
      # actually answered, by a reader, before deciding to conclude.
      given("an interview that asked nothing has discovered nothing") { asked.value.positive? }

      emits "InterviewConcluded"
    end

    command "Abandon" do
      role "Interviewer"
      goal "End a session whose output should not be trusted"

      reference_to Interview
      emits "InterviewAbandoned"
    end

    query "Gathering" do
      description "Sessions live right now — the ones a runner may still ask into."
      where(status: "gathering")
      order_by :session
    end

    query "ForChapter" do
      description "Every session ever held against one chapter — how a chapter's shape was arrived at."
      attribute :chapter, ChapterName
      where(chapter: :chapter)
      order_by :session
    end
  end

  # THE CATALOGUE IS DATA, NOT A CLOSED SET — and this aggregate exists to
  # record that decision.
  #
  # A `one_of` was the obvious first move: the practice's standing
  # questions, declared once, refused if not a member. That is exactly
  # backwards. A closed set refuses what is not in it, and a catalogue's
  # entire reason to exist is that it LEARNS — the question you wish you had
  # asked on the last project is the most valuable thing to come out of it,
  # and a catalogue that cannot accept it costs a framework edit to write
  # down. `QuestionSource` above is the contrast: three layers, fixed by the
  # design of the interview itself. The set that never grows is a set; the
  # set that must grow is a table.
  #
  # The cost, stated plainly: this needs seeding. A fresh runtime has an
  # empty catalogue and every question comes from `mechanical` or
  # `judgment` until somebody drafts one.
  aggregate "StandingQuestion" do
    description "One question the practice always asks, independent of any session — the catalogue, held as records so it can learn."

    identified_by { slug.value }

    attribute :slug,   Slug
    attribute :prompt, Prompt
    attribute :topic,  Topic
    attribute :why,    Rationale

    # A STABLE HANDLE A QUESTION CAN CITE. Not the prompt text — a prompt
    # gets reworded, and `Question#catalogue_ref` must keep pointing at the
    # same entry when it does.
    value_object "Slug" do
      attribute :value, String
      invariant("a standing question is slugged") { !value.to_s.empty? }
    end

    value_object "Prompt" do
      attribute :value, String
      invariant("a standing question asks something") { !value.to_s.empty? }
    end

    value_object "Topic" do
      attribute :value, String
      invariant("a topic is named") { !value.to_s.empty? }
    end

    # WHY THE PRACTICE ASKS IT — required, and that is the point. A
    # catalogue nobody can defend entry by entry becomes a checklist, and a
    # checklist is what an interview is supposed to replace.
    value_object "Rationale" do
      attribute :value, String
      invariant("a standing question says why it is asked") { !value.to_s.empty? }
    end

    lifecycle :status, default: "drafted" do
      transition "Publish" => "published", from: "drafted"
      # REWORDING UN-PUBLISHES IT, the same rule Finding's `Amend` carries
      # one aggregate down: the publication was of the OLD wording, and
      # carrying it forward would let a reviewed decision be quietly
      # attached to text nobody reviewed.
      transition "Reword"  => "drafted",   from: ["drafted", "published"]
      transition "Retire"  => "retired",   from: "published"
    end

    command "Draft" do
      role "Catalogue steward"
      goal "Write down a question the practice should always ask"

      attribute :slug,   Slug
      attribute :prompt, Prompt
      attribute :topic,  Topic
      attribute :why,    Rationale

      emits "StandingQuestionDrafted"
    end

    command "Publish" do
      role "Catalogue steward"
      goal "Put a question into circulation for every future session"

      reference_to StandingQuestion
      emits "StandingQuestionPublished"
    end

    command "Reword" do
      role "Catalogue steward"
      goal "Change what a standing question actually says"

      reference_to StandingQuestion
      attribute :prompt, Prompt
      attribute :why,    Rationale

      then_set :prompt, to: :prompt
      then_set :why,    to: :why

      emits "StandingQuestionReworded"
    end

    command "Retire" do
      role "Catalogue steward"
      goal "Stop asking a question that stopped being useful"

      reference_to StandingQuestion
      emits "StandingQuestionRetired"
    end

    query "Published" do
      description "The catalogue a session opens from, in topic order."
      where(status: "published")
      order_by :topic
    end

    query "ForTopic" do
      description "The standing questions for one area, when a session narrows to it."
      attribute :topic, Topic
      where(status: "published")
      where(topic: :topic)
      order_by :slug
    end
  end

  # AN AGGREGATE, AND `reference_to` DECIDED IT.
  #
  # A finding looks exactly like a Question does — established inside a
  # session, meaningless without it, which is the entity case. It is not
  # one, for a mechanical reason that overrides the taste: a Proposal must
  # POINT AT the finding it rests on, and a reference reaches heads only
  # (`entity_builder` declares no `reference_to` at all). Anything that must
  # be pointed at is a head.
  #
  # The taste agrees once it is pushed. A finding OUTLIVES its session: the
  # second interview against a chapter opens with what the first one
  # established, and amends it. A question does not survive its session; a
  # fact does.
  aggregate "Finding" do
    description "One thing now known about the domain, the answers it came from, and whether anybody has agreed to it."

    reference_to Interview

    # ONE PATH, not `interview_id` plus a sequence. A Proposal references
    # this, and a reference to a composite-identity head is a shape nothing
    # in the corpus exercises. The caller mints `reference` before
    # dispatching — the same concern, stated the same way, that
    # identity.bluebook's `Register` leaves to its caller.
    identified_by { reference.value }

    attribute :reference, FindingReference
    # NOT PART OF THE IDENTITY — held so findings read back in the order
    # they were established rather than whatever order a store iterates.
    attribute :sequence,  FindingSequence
    attribute :topic,     Topic
    attribute :statement, FindingStatement
    attribute :evidence,  list_of(Evidence)
    attribute :reason,    Reason

    value_object "FindingReference" do
      attribute :value, String
      invariant("a finding is referenced") { !value.to_s.empty? }
    end

    value_object "FindingSequence" do
      attribute :value, Integer
      invariant("a finding sequence is positive") { value.positive? }
    end

    value_object "Topic" do
      attribute :value, String
      invariant("a topic is named") { !value.to_s.empty? }
    end

    # `FindingStatement`, NOT `Statement`, AND THE PREFIX IS LOAD-BEARING.
    #
    # `Facade::Surface.install` installs EVERY aggregate name as a TOP-LEVEL
    # constant on Object (see const_shim.rb's own note, which documents the
    # same hazard from the other side). Banking declares `aggregate
    # "Statement"`, so the moment banking has booted anywhere in the
    # process, bare `Statement` resolves to `Banking::Statement` — a real
    # Ruby constant — instead of reaching `const_missing` and being read as
    # a name. This chapter then refuses with "no ValueObject ... named
    # Interview:Finding:Banking::Statement", and only when some other
    # domain happens to have booted first: green alone, red in the suite.
    #
    # Measured, not feared — it is exactly how this file first went red.
    # Prefixing is what banking already does for the same reason
    # (`AccountNumber`, `TransferReference`), and this aggregate's own
    # `FindingReference`/`FindingSequence` were already spelled that way.
    value_object "FindingStatement" do
      attribute :value, String
      invariant("a finding states something") { !value.to_s.empty? }
    end

    # WHICH ANSWERS THIS RESTS ON — question numbers within the interview
    # above, so a reader can walk from a fact back to the words that
    # produced it. A list, because a fact is routinely the join of two
    # answers given twenty minutes apart, and the pair is the evidence, not
    # either half.
    #
    # A plain value object, not an entity: nothing addresses one citation,
    # changes it, or gives it a lifecycle. Appended and read back — the
    # first of the three cases, and the one a pizza's toppings are.
    value_object "Evidence" do
      attribute :question, Integer
    end

    value_object "QuestionNumber" do
      attribute :value, Integer
      invariant("a cited question is numbered from one") { value.positive? }
    end

    value_object "Reason" do
      attribute :value, String
    end

    value_object "InterviewRef" do
      attribute :value, String
    end

    lifecycle :status, default: "asserted" do
      transition "Confirm" => "confirmed", from: "asserted"
      # AMENDING DROPS A CONFIRMATION. The respondent agreed to the old
      # wording; carrying that agreement onto new wording is how an
      # inference ends up wearing a human's signature.
      transition "Amend"   => "asserted",  from: ["asserted", "confirmed"]
      transition "Retract" => "retracted", from: ["asserted", "confirmed"]
    end

    # `asserted` IS THE DEFAULT, AND THAT IS THE POINT. A finding comes into
    # being as something the interviewer BELIEVES it heard — not as a fact.
    # `confirmed` is the respondent saying yes. The line between those two
    # is the line between a domain model and a plausible-sounding one, and
    # it is the only line in this chapter a machine cannot cross on its own.
    command "Establish" do
      role "Interviewer"
      goal "Write down something the answers appear to have settled"

      reference_to Interview
      attribute :reference, FindingReference
      attribute :sequence,  FindingSequence
      attribute :topic,     Topic
      attribute :statement, FindingStatement

      emits "FindingEstablished"
    end

    # NOT A LIFECYCLE TRANSITION, on purpose — adding supporting evidence to
    # a confirmed finding must not un-confirm it the way `Amend`
    # deliberately does, so this cannot be a transition into `asserted`.
    # Left off the machine entirely it could fire against a retracted
    # finding, so the rule is a `given` instead — the shape `LogVisit` uses
    # against a box that must be rented.
    command "Cite" do
      role "Interviewer"
      goal "Name another answer this finding rests on"

      reference_to Finding
      attribute :question, QuestionNumber

      given("evidence is cited against a finding that still stands") { status != "retracted" }

      then_set :evidence, append: { question: :question }

      emits "EvidenceCited"
    end

    command "Confirm" do
      role "Respondent"
      goal "Agree that this is actually true of the domain"

      reference_to Finding
      emits "FindingConfirmed"
    end

    command "Amend" do
      role "Interviewer"
      goal "Restate a finding that was nearly right"

      reference_to Finding
      attribute :statement, FindingStatement

      then_set :statement, to: :statement

      emits "FindingAmended"
    end

    command "Retract" do
      role "Architect"
      goal "Withdraw a fact a later answer contradicted"

      reference_to Finding
      attribute :reason, Reason

      then_set :reason, to: :reason

      emits "FindingRetracted"
    end

    query "EstablishedIn" do
      description "Everything one session settled, in the order it was settled."
      attribute :interview_id, InterviewRef
      where(interview_id: :interview_id)
      order_by :sequence
    end

    query "ByTopic" do
      description "What has been said about one area, across every session that ever asked."
      attribute :topic, Topic
      where(topic: :topic)
      order_by :sequence
    end

    query "Unconfirmed" do
      description "What the interviewer believes and nobody has agreed to yet — the shortest useful list in the chapter."
      where(status: "asserted")
      order_by :sequence
    end
  end

  # THE THING THAT WOULD BE DISPATCHED, held as a record before it is.
  #
  # AN AGGREGATE, AND THE LIST DECIDED IT. A proposal carries the arguments
  # of the dispatch it would make, and arguments are an open map — an entity
  # cannot hold a `list_of` at all (`Coercion.hydrate_entity_list` reaches
  # for `aggregate.entities`, which an entity has none of), and no value
  # object can hold a map. `Wiring` in world.bluebook is its own root for
  # precisely this reason and says so in its own description.
  #
  # WHAT IT IS NOT: a model of the change. There is no `Attribute` or
  # `Aggregate` in this chapter. A proposal names a VERB and a set of
  # ARGUMENTS, and the meta-domain is what knows what those mean — the
  # difference between a record of an intent and a second, private copy of
  # the language.
  aggregate "Proposal" do
    description "One change to the bluebook under construction — the verb it would dispatch, the arguments it would carry, and whether anybody accepted it."

    reference_to Interview
    # A PROPOSAL RESTS ON A FINDING, and this is required rather than
    # optional. A change nobody can trace to something the respondent said
    # is a guess, and a guess that reaches the meta-domain looks identical
    # to a fact once it is in there. This reference is what makes "no change
    # without a reason" a consequence of the model instead of a rule
    # somebody remembers.
    reference_to Finding

    identified_by { reference.value }

    attribute :reference, ProposalReference
    attribute :sequence,  ProposalSequence
    attribute :verb,      MetaVerb
    attribute :rationale, Rationale
    attribute :arguments, list_of(Argument)
    attribute :refusal,   RefusalText
    attribute :reason,    CorrectionReason

    value_object "ProposalReference" do
      attribute :value, String
      invariant("a proposal is referenced") { !value.to_s.empty? }
    end

    value_object "ProposalSequence" do
      attribute :value, Integer
      invariant("a proposal sequence is positive") { value.positive? }
    end

    # THE VERB, SHAPED RATHER THAN ENUMERATED. `Bluebook::Aggregate.Attribute`,
    # `Bluebook::ValueObject.Field` — the meta-domain declares dozens and
    # grows, so a closed set here would refuse the language's own next word.
    # What CAN be said is the shape, and the language already says it: the
    # wording of its own `not_fully_qualified` refusal is "{verb} is not a
    # fully-qualified verb (Domain::Aggregate.Command)", which is this
    # pattern written as a sentence.
    #
    # Spelled with explicit ranges rather than \w, because the perl classes
    # flip between ASCII and Unicode across regex engines — see
    # PatternSubset, which refuses them when the bluebook is declared rather
    # than letting two readers disagree about a proposal later.
    value_object "MetaVerb" do
      attribute :value, String, pattern: '^[A-Za-z]+::[A-Za-z]+\.[A-Za-z]+$'
    end

    value_object "Rationale" do
      attribute :value, String
      invariant("a proposal says why it is being made") { !value.to_s.empty? }
    end

    # AN OPEN MAP, AS ROWS — the shape this language already reaches for
    # three separate times: `Query::AskOption`, `Wiring::Setting`, and a
    # closed set's `member` pairs. Every one of them is a list on a HEAD,
    # for the same reason: no value object can hold a map.
    #
    #   `name`  — the keyword the meta command declares: "name", "type".
    #   `field` — the field INSIDE the value object that keyword expects:
    #             almost always "value", because almost every attribute of
    #             the meta-domain is a single-field value object.
    #   `value` — what goes there.
    #
    # `field` IS EMPTY FOR A REFERENCE ARGUMENT, and that is not a gap. A
    # reference is an id, not an object, and an id has no field to open.
    value_object "Argument" do
      attribute :name,  String
      attribute :field, String
      attribute :value, String
    end

    value_object "ArgumentName" do
      attribute :value, String
      invariant("an argument is named") { !value.to_s.empty? }
    end

    value_object "ArgumentField" do
      attribute :value, String
    end

    value_object "ArgumentValue" do
      attribute :value, String
    end

    # WHAT THE META-DOMAIN SAID WHEN IT REFUSED. No `given` on `Refused`
    # asking whether this is present — the invariant fires during coercion,
    # before any given is reached, so a predicate here could never be the
    # one to refuse.
    value_object "RefusalText" do
      attribute :value, String
      invariant("a refusal says what was wrong") { !value.to_s.empty? }
    end

    value_object "InterviewRef" do
      attribute :value, String
    end

    # WHY A WRITTEN MISTAKE STILL NEEDS A WORD FOR ITSELF. `written` is a
    # true terminal for `Supersede`/`Reject` on purpose (see the lifecycle's
    # own comment below) — but "the meta-domain took it" and "it was the
    # right thing to have asked for" are different facts, and a real
    # interview conflates them at its own risk. `bin/interview propose`'s
    # own worked history found this the hard way: a wrong verb
    # (`Command.Argument` where `Command.Reference` belonged) and a field
    # name that collided with a lifecycle's own auto-created attribute both
    # dispatched cleanly — the meta-domain had no rule to refuse either
    # one — and only broke the SAVED domain, downstream, permanently. Fixing
    # either meant hand-editing rendered source, outside every tool this
    # chapter has. `Correct` is what should have been there instead: the
    # mistake stays written, in full, in the log — nothing here rewrites it
    # — but `rebuild`/`Writer` skip a corrected proposal on replay, so its
    # effect stops reaching the model without a line of source ever being
    # hand-edited again.
    value_object "CorrectionReason" do
      attribute :value, String
      invariant("a correction says what was wrong") { !value.to_s.empty? }
    end

    lifecycle :status, default: "proposed" do
      # A SELF-TRANSITION so arguments can only be attached while the
      # proposal is still open — the trick `Ask` uses one aggregate up, and
      # for the same reason: a proposal that grows an argument after
      # somebody accepted it is not the proposal they accepted.
      transition "Supply"    => "proposed",   from: "proposed"
      transition "Accept"    => "accepted",   from: "proposed"
      transition "Reject"    => "rejected",   from: "proposed"
      transition "Supersede" => "superseded", from: "proposed"
      # THE SPLIT THAT MATTERS MOST IN THIS CHAPTER.
      #
      # "Written on accept" taken literally would make `accepted` terminal.
      # It must not be. Accepting is what causes a DISPATCH into the
      # meta-domain, and that dispatch can be refused by the language's own
      # rules — `Aggregate.Seal` refuses a head that says nothing about what
      # it is known by, `Identify` refuses a path reaching no scalar, an
      # attribute naming an undeclared value object fails reference
      # resolution outright. Every one of those is a real refusal a real
      # proposal will hit.
      #
      # Without these two states a proposal reads "accepted" whether the
      # meta-domain took it or spat it back, and the record silently stops
      # matching the model — the most dangerous failure this chapter can
      # have, because both sides look fine.
      #
      # `Supersede` deliberately does NOT leave `accepted`. Once a change is
      # written, a later change is a new proposal, not a rewriting of the
      # decision that produced this one.
      transition "Written"   => "written",    from: "accepted"
      transition "Refused"   => "refused",    from: "accepted"

      # NOT a second `Supersede` under another name. `Supersede` retires a
      # proposal nobody ruled on yet — the decision never happened.
      # `Correct` retires a proposal the meta-domain DID take, on the
      # discovery that taking it was a mistake — the decision happened and
      # is still true history; only its EFFECT stops being replayed. See
      # `CorrectionReason`'s own comment for why this exists.
      transition "Correct"   => "corrected",  from: "written"
    end

    command "Propose" do
      role "Interviewer"
      goal "Put forward one change the findings argue for"

      reference_to Interview
      reference_to Finding
      attribute :reference, ProposalReference
      attribute :sequence,  ProposalSequence
      attribute :verb,      MetaVerb
      attribute :rationale, Rationale

      emits "ChangeProposed"
    end

    # ONE ARGUMENT AT A TIME, which is how the meta-domain itself is written
    # — `Command.Argument`, `Query.Option` are each a verb attaching one
    # row, because a list cannot be carried by one argument and every other
    # list in that language is filled by a verb of its own.
    command "Supply" do
      role "Interviewer"
      goal "Give one argument the dispatch will carry"

      reference_to Proposal
      attribute :name,  ArgumentName
      attribute :field, ArgumentField
      attribute :value, ArgumentValue

      then_set :arguments, append: { name: :name, field: :field, value: :value }

      emits "ArgumentSupplied"
    end

    command "Accept" do
      role "Architect"
      goal "Agree to a change, which is what causes it to be dispatched"

      reference_to Proposal
      emits "ChangeAccepted"
    end

    command "Reject" do
      role "Architect"
      goal "Refuse a change on its merits"

      reference_to Proposal
      emits "ChangeRejected"
    end

    command "Supersede" do
      role "Interviewer"
      goal "Retire a proposal a later one replaces, before anybody ruled on it"

      reference_to Proposal
      emits "ChangeSuperseded"
    end

    # THE RUNNER REPORTING BACK. This chapter does not dispatch into the
    # meta-domain and cannot — see the closing note on why there is no
    # process manager. The caller performs the dispatch and says which way
    # it went, exactly the way identity.bluebook leaves minting an id to its
    # caller and says so rather than pretending otherwise.
    command "Written" do
      role "System"
      goal "Record that the meta-domain took the dispatch"

      reference_to Proposal
      emits "ChangeWritten"
    end

    command "Refused" do
      role "System"
      goal "Record that the meta-domain refused the dispatch, and what it said"

      reference_to Proposal
      attribute :refusal, RefusalText

      then_set :refusal, to: :refusal

      emits "ChangeRefused"
    end

    command "Correct" do
      role "Interviewer"
      goal "Mark a written proposal as a mistake, excluded from replay, without erasing that it was written"

      reference_to Proposal
      attribute :reason, CorrectionReason

      then_set :reason, to: :reason

      emits "ChangeCorrected"
    end

    query "Pending" do
      description "One session's undecided proposals, in the order they were made — the accept/reject queue."
      attribute :interview_id, InterviewRef
      where(interview_id: :interview_id)
      where(status: "proposed")
      order_by :sequence
    end

    query "Awaiting" do
      description "Accepted and not yet dispatched. The list that must always empty — anything sitting here is a decision the model does not reflect."
      where(status: "accepted")
      order_by :sequence
    end

    # A DOTTED WHERE, HOPPING THROUGH A REFERENCE — and it is here because
    # the rule it enforces CANNOT be a `given`. "A proposal may only rest on
    # a confirmed finding" reads like a precondition, but a reference is an
    # id at dispatch time, not the record it points at: `finding.status`
    # resolves to nothing inside a predicate. Only a where-clause hops. So
    # the rule is not enforced, it is SURFACED — which is the honest thing,
    # since a proposal made ahead of a confirmation is legitimate and merely
    # must not be accepted while it stands there.
    query "RestingOnUnconfirmed" do
      description "Live proposals whose finding nobody has agreed to yet — accepting one writes an inference into the model as though it were a fact."
      where(status: "proposed")
      where(:"finding.status" => "asserted")
      order_by :sequence
    end

    query "RestingOnRetracted" do
      description "Live proposals whose finding was withdrawn. The other list that must always empty, and the reason a retraction is not a policy."
      where(status: "proposed")
      where(:"finding.status" => "retracted")
      order_by :sequence
    end
  end

  # NO POLICY, AND NO PROCESS MANAGER. Both siblings in this directory
  # declare none either, but here it is a decision rather than an absence,
  # and two candidates were written out and taken back:
  #
  # ON "FindingRetracted", TRIGGER "Proposal.Supersede". Real in intent — a
  # withdrawn fact must not leave live proposals standing — and wrong in
  # mechanism: a policy delivers ONE dispatch, and a single retraction
  # routinely strands several proposals. It would retire one of them and
  # read, in the log, as though it had handled all of them, which is worse
  # than doing nothing. `RestingOnRetracted` is the honest answer, and it is
  # a list a human closes.
  #
  # A SAGA OVER accepted -> written | refused. A genuine multi-step protocol
  # and exactly the shape `Settlement` has — except the middle step
  # dispatches into a DIFFERENT runtime carrying arguments assembled at run
  # time from `Proposal#arguments`. A saga's `with:` is written into the
  # source, statically, one key at a time. A saga that cannot carry the
  # payload can only pretend to be driving, and the reason `Settlement`
  # earns its place is that it actually does.
end
